Z||000009||varnster||02-14-2001||06:55 PM||||Look on spending around 350 on a good sub and<BR>a 2 channel amp. I just put a JL AUDIO 8" <BR>micro sub in my 87 gt, its got a very small<BR>enclosure its not square either its shaped like a half circle it is even ported. fits<BR>very nice bolted to the rear firewall behind the pass seat, im using a sony explode 2 channel bridged mono 150x1 it is more than enough power for an 8" and crutchfield has<BR>them on sell for 119.00 the sub goes for around 200. this setup sounds wonderfull.<BR>just enough bass and it doesnt feel like someone is kicking the back of your seat||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||02-14-2001||07:12 PM||||no, you don't need to look to spend $350 for a good sub and amp. let me back up for a second. exactly how much are you willing to spend? it's not going to be cheap. don't think you're gonna get away spending $100 on a nice setup.<P>you say you want to go with a rockford punch 8" sub. any reasons for choosing this sub?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||02-14-2001||07:19 PM||||If you are going to use an 8" sub you will need and amp. There is no way around it. The head unit cannot put out the amps quick enough that is required to push the sub correctly. Especially when the head unit is driving other speakers too. Most 8" subs want 20 to 30 watts minimum. That would be driving the rest of the speakers pretty hard. The most you are probably putting to the speakers when it's "cranked" in reality is 15 to 20 watts. Most head units start to distort after that. The head units are usually rated at max power out. The distortion level is usually measured at 1/3 power. If you want to do it easily, go onto Ebay or Yahoo auctions and look for a powered Bazooka tube. The amp is built in and even the 6 1/2" tube will pound some bass into the car. You can also look for an amp. Something that puts out 100W or better and can be set to mono or bridged to 1 channel. The amp I am using for my sub was $50.00 on Yahoo and it puts out 200W bridged.<BR>You will notice a world of improvement. Even the "generic" 8" you have will sound better.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||varnster||02-14-2001||07:36 PM||||sqoach, let me clarify the 350 bucks<BR>IM TALKING NEW ITEMS IN A PROPERLY TUNED<BR>ENCLOSURE, WITH A HALFASS WARRENTY.if you<BR>can get new NAMEBRAND products, including <BR>properly guaged wire, fuses and RCA'S, and<BR>a amp to push it,for less,  ill take a hundred of each item and make a profit<BR>selling it on the street.||||reg||1
Z||000013||sqoach||02-14-2001||07:52 PM||||i know what you were talking about varnster. i was just saying you don't need to spend $350 to get a good setup. i paid $170 for a new mtx audio (well-known name brand (not that it matters that much)) 10" sub rated at 500 peak, 250 rms, and a new mtx audio 250x1 bridged amp. i haven't yet got the wires and box, but it will add about another $30 at the most for me.<P>just because it's a name brand, doesn't mean it's the best. you definately spend more this way, but a lot of that money is spent on the name. one of my friends that i have talked about before, spent over $1,500 on a rockford fosgate setup. 2 dvc hx2 10s, a box for both of them, 800.2 power amp, 800.2 punch amp, and all rockford fosgate wiring. he also bought an extra battery to aid in the power supply.<P>i don't want this to turn into a flame war or anything. just saying you don't have to waste money when you don't have to. shop around. find the deals. don't get pissed at me cuz i said you don't need to spend that much. just trying to help someone out that is on a tight budget.||||reg||1
