Z||000016||sqoach||02-14-2001||08:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by varnster:<BR><B>youre right man , I feel like an idiot,<BR>you can spend less and get a good set up<BR>some of us care a little to much for those<BR>sounds the human ear cant even hear.SORRY<BR>CANN YOU FORGIVE. IT IS VALLENTINES DAY.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>no problem. i just hate to see anything get out of hand. i always just try to find good deals on stuff. no hard feelings. (i assume you meant what you said and were not being sarcastic)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||sqoach||02-14-2001||08:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by anthony_86gt:<BR><B>I want to get the rockford because thats what a friend of mine has.  which amp should i get?? i just need to power the one sub so i dont need a real good one.  lets say i want to spend around 200.  i already have a box, all i need is the sub(i found for $50) and an amp. what about just a crappy jenson to power my sub. how many watts does the amp need and is it hard to hook up(amp)???</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>it sounds like you're new to the audio world. the one your friend has must sound pretty good since you want to get it. and that's not a bad price either. now you need an amp. a 100-watt amp should do just fine. and for a bit of info, jensen makes some pretty good amps. they're not crappy at all. my friend that i was just talking about use to have a 400-watt jensen amp pushing 2 JLAudio 12s. and it sounded pretty good. i was surprised how well that amp pushed those subs. each sub was rated at 300 watts rms power, so they could take a lot more power than what he had rigged up.<P>they're not that hard to hook up. it should come with instructions to show you how. and if you have trouble, just ask for help.<BR>oreif knows a lot more about this kind of stuff than i do. he can tell you exactly how to hook it all up.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||varnster||02-14-2001||08:45 PM||||You can get a good amp for that price range.<BR>never used jenson. ive been getting sony amps<BR>for my fiero, but in my explorer I got orion<BR>amps, look in crutchfield mag or there web site, like I said the 2 channel sony xm-552hx<BR>is what im using and in my opinion a good value at 119 bucks, whatever you get you need<BR>at least 100watts rms bridged, mono prefered<BR>with a low pass crossover,allso RCA inputs<BR>instead of speaker level inputs, due to speaker level inputs tend to pick up some<BR>noise, not allways but normally RCA inputs<BR>are cleaner.hope this helps<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Oreif||02-14-2001||08:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by anthony_86gt:<BR><B>I want to get the rockford because thats what a friend of mine has.  which amp should i get?? i just need to power the one sub so i dont need a real good one.  lets say i want to spend around 200.  i already have a box, all i need is the sub(i found for $50) and an amp. what about just a crappy jenson to power my sub. how many watts does the amp need and is it hard to hook up(amp)???</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>To hook up an amp is easy. You will also need to get a set of RCA cables. These will plug into the back of your head unit and plug into the amp. Make sure the amp is bridgable to 1 channel. Usually there is a switch to switch the output. Radio Shack sells an Optimus amp that is 170W bridged for $59.99. It even has bass/treble control. Just turn the treble all the way down and the bass at 1/2 way. Then you need to hook up 3 wires. 1 goes to the battery with a fuse (included with the amp) then ground. The third wire goes to the back of the head unit. It will be either orange or blue. Usually says "aux power" or "remote". This is the wire that will turn the amp on only when the head unit is on. You can mount the amp above the box behind the passenger seat with the sub. Then once everything is hooked up, get some music with real heavy bass. On the amp is a knob that says "gain" turn this all the way down. Then crank up the volume until the front speakers are loudest. Adjust the bass on the head unit so that you don't distort the speakers or have a "popping" sound. Then reach back and turn the gain on the amp. adjust it so the bass sounds great (usually your preference). Now when you listen to music everything should be set. If the song has less bass and you want more, Just use the bass control on the head unit. It will increase the bass to the sub. If you go with a different amp you could also get one that has a crossover built in and you won't need the bass/treble control. Any other questions just let me know.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||sqoach||02-14-2001||08:58 PM||||check this out:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.millionbuy.com/orncs1002.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.millionbuy.com/orncs1002.html</A> <P>there's a nice orion amp that will put out plenty of power for that sub. and not too expensive either. actually that's a pretty good deal.||||reg||1
Z||000021||sqoach||02-14-2001||09:31 PM||||or if you want to get a rockford amp this one would be perfect:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/product.asp?sku=10221" TARGET=_blank>http://www.onlinecarstereo.com/caraudio/product.asp?sku=10221</A> <P>those rockford amps are really nice. <p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 02-14-2001).]||||reg||1
